const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What is the function of an aerosol actuator?",
    a: "An aerosol actuator is the molded component mounted on the valve stem that a user presses to open the valve and dispense the pressurized formulation. It shapes and directs the spray by forcing the fluid through internal channels and a discharge orifice, converting stored propellant pressure into a controlled mist, stream, or foam."
  },
  {
    q: "How does orifice size affect aerosol spray performance?",
    a: "The actuator orifice is the final restriction the formulation passes through before release, and its diameter governs exit velocity and atomization. Smaller orifices raise fluid velocity and produce finer droplets but increase susceptibility to clogging and pressure sensitivity, while larger orifices lower velocity and shear, producing coarser sprays or streams with higher mass flow rates."
  },
  {
    q: "What materials are aerosol actuators made from?",
    a: "Aerosol actuators are almost universally injection-molded from polypropylene or polyethylene because both polymers resist chemical attack from common propellants and solvents, maintain dimensional stability under repeated valve-stem loading, and tolerate the stress-cracking risk posed by hydrocarbon propellants such as LPG and DME."
  },
  {
    q: "What causes an aerosol actuator to clog?",
    a: "Clogging commonly results from an orifice diameter too small for the formulation's viscosity or particulate content, from dried residue accumulating in the discharge channel between uses, or from an actuator-formulation pairing that was not validated during pilot testing. Incoming inspection and functional testing with the actual formulation catch this risk before commercial launch."
  },
  {
    q: "Why do different aerosol products use different actuator types?",
    a: "Actuator geometry must match both the formulation's physical properties and the application's performance requirements. A wide fan spray suits coatings that need broad, even coverage, a narrow stream suits precision cleaning or lubrication, and a metered-dose actuator suits pharmaceutical products where a fixed, repeatable dose volume is a regulatory requirement."
  },
  {
    q: "Can an aerosol actuator be used with any valve or can?",
    a: "No. Actuator selection must account for the valve's stem diameter and flow rate, the propellant's vapor pressure, and the formulation's viscosity, since these factors jointly determine spray pattern and discharge rate. Copying an actuator design from another product without validating it against the specific valve and formulation combination is a common cause of aerosol product failures."
  }
];

